#{[17592186445110 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445110 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445110 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445110 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445110 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+1.2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${x}^{′}$ in the first equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445110 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9a5577e-11c5-5655-a180-370cf60950eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445110 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=4x-2xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{prey}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $4x$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-2xy$ term)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445111 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445111 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445111 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+7xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=6y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "deb74bf3-ea72-561b-8b82-34ede161ce61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445111 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy=0 \\\\ 6y-5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy & =0 \\\\ x(-4+7y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{4}{7}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y-5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{4}{7},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y-5xy & =0 \\\\ y(6-5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}(6-5x) & =0 \\\\ 6-5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{6}{5},\\frac{4}{7}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{6}{5},\\frac{4}{7}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
